  "preamble": "Lowering of the near-close near-front [ɪ] to close-mid [e], usually as a merger. When Latin lost phonemic vowel length by the 5th century, short ĭ survived as a lax [ɪ], distinct from long ī [i] and long ē [e]. Towards the end of the Roman Empire /ɪ/ merged with /e/ in most regions, though not in Africa or a few peripheral areas of Italy. Latin pilus [ˈpɪ.ɫʊs] 'hair' thus became Italian and Spanish pelo, and siccus 'dry' became Italian secco and Spanish seco. Short ŭ [ʊ] merged with ō as close /o/ in parallel.",
